//
// A CONVERSION OUT OF ANOTHER SOURCE'S RECORD IS REFUSED (`@FR-Op-Conv`).  A conversion between two
// foreign types, and one from a foreign record into a built-in, belong to the source that owns the
// record.  Each function is one refusal with its expectation.
//
// ── a-conversion-between-two-foreign-types-is-refused ──
//
// A CONVERSION BETWEEN TWO FOREIGN TYPES IS REFUSED (`@FR-Op-Conv` crossed with `@FR-Op-Home`).
// `text → OcPt`: the `self` is built in and the target is another library's, so neither end is a
// type of this source — Op-Conv's exception (a conversion INTO this source's type) does not apply
// and Op-Home refuses the definition where it is written, whatever uses it.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; refused as the rule says, interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both types.
// HOLDS: `a-conversion-into-a-type-of-this-source-takes-any-self` beside it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `operator to_oc_pt` converts `text` to `OcPt`, and neither is a type of this package
//
// ── a-conversion-from-a-foreign-record-into-a-built-in-is-refused ──
//
// A CONVERSION FROM A FOREIGN RECORD INTO A BUILT-IN IS REFUSED (`@FR-Op-Conv` crossed with
// `@FR-Op-Home`).  `OcPt → integer`: the `self` is another library's record and the target is built
// in — the conversion belongs to `OcPt`'s own source, so here it is refused at the definition.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; refused as the rule says, interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both types.
// HOLDS: `a-conversion-into-a-type-of-this-source-takes-any-self` beside it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `operator to_integer` converts `OcPt` to `integer`, and neither is a type of this package

use op_conv_fixture_lib::*;


operator to_oc_pt(self: text) -> OcPt { OcPt { x: len(self), y: 0 } }



operator to_integer(self: OcPt) -> integer { self.x }

